# Builder fragment: the studies share the helpers declared in build.py.
mega='<div class="mega-demo"><div class="mega-nav"><span>vestre</span><b>PRODUCTS ⌄</b></div><div class="mega-sheet"><div class="mega-content"><div><small>PRODUCTS</small><p>Seating<br>Tables<br>Planters<br>Accessories</p></div><div class="mini-scene"><span class="bench"></span></div><div><small>SERIES</small><p>GLADE<br>BLOC<br>APRIL</p></div></div></div></div>'
section('mega','A fade, without a cascade.','''<p>Desktop navigation opens on hover or keyboard focus. The mega-menu container transitions opacity for 320ms using the header’s <code>(.3,.6,0,1)</code> curve. Its transition declaration includes transform, but the inspected states do not change transform. There is no item-by-item stagger.</p><p>The sheet is 40svh high, bounded by 288–600px. Closing removes the active content immediately while the empty container fades. That lifecycle matters: simply reversing an animation with its content still present would produce a different result. Pointer leave, focus leaving the header, Escape and route changes close it.</p>'''+lab('mega','Menu opacity and content lifecycle','The viewport-scaled height is compressed to fit this study. Switch to Close to inspect the immediate removal of content.',mega,320,channel('content','Apply source content lifecycle'),'<div class="controls"><label>Direction <select data-mega-mode><option value="open">Open</option><option value="close">Close</option></select></label></div>')+recipe('mega',['b664f27371261aee.js']))
accents='<div class="accent-layout"><div class="accent-label">PRODUCTS <span class="accent-caret">⌄</span><i class="accent-line"></i></div><div class="market-wrap"><span class="market-trigger">US / EN ⌄</span><div class="market-popup"><span>Choose market</span><b>United States ✓</b><b>Europe</b><b>United Kingdom</b></div></div></div>'
section('accents','Small controls have their own tempo.','''<p>The navigation underline grows from 0% to 100% width in 150ms; the caret rotates 180° in 200ms. Colors and borders generally transition over 150ms. These use Tailwind’s default <code>(.4,0,.2,1)</code>, a different curve from the header.</p><p>The market selector uses Base UI state attributes to animate opacity 0→1 and scale .95→1 over 150ms, with a top transform origin. The study shows its visual states without changing the site’s locale.</p>'''+lab('accents','Underline, caret and market popup','Scrub to compare the 150ms and 200ms clocks. These changes are coordinated by interaction state, not by an entrance timeline.',accents,200,channel('line','Underline')+channel('caret','Caret')+channel('market','Popup'))+recipe('accents',['b664f27371261aee.js','a0955bf8ab7df94c.css']))
product='<div class="product-demo"><div class="product-studio"><div class="seat-art"><i></i><b></b></div></div><div class="product-life"><div class="land-lines"></div><div class="seat-art"><i></i><b></b></div></div><p class="product-caption">FARI <span>Product caption</span></p></div>'
section('products','Replace the context, not the scale.','''<p>Product cards stack two images. On hover the contained studio image fades out, the cropped lifestyle image fades in, and the caption fades out. All three opacity transitions take 300ms using Tailwind <code>ease-out = (0,0,.2,1)</code>. The card does not enlarge its image.</p><p>The live Fari card reached opacity 0/1 for the two images and 0 for its caption during pointer hover. All eight current product cards carry this pattern. Shared-layout badge springs occur in delivered product modules, but no active homepage badges were observed; they are excluded here.</p>'''+lab('products','Studio → lifestyle crossfade','Abstract artwork substitutes for the source photography. Isolate either layer to see why a crossfade needs both images stacked in one frame.',product,300,channel('studio','Studio fade out')+channel('life','Lifestyle fade in')+channel('caption','Caption fade out'),'<div class="controls"><label>Direction <select data-product-mode><option value="in">Hover in</option><option value="out">Hover out</option></select></label></div>')+recipe('products',['dddb31d6982abe9f.js','a38911ff8b6091cb.js']))
hover='<div class="hover-frame"><div class="hover-art"><div class="land-lines"></div><span class="bench"></span></div><div class="hover-overlay"></div><span class="hover-label">SERIES / BLOC</span></div>'
section('media','Four small zoom recipes.','''<p>The series videos scale 1→1.03 over 500ms. Article thumbnails start slightly cropped at 1.02 and reach 1.04 over 300ms. Project/reference images use 1→1.02 over 300ms. Mega-menu series thumbnails use 1→1.04 over 500ms, accompanied by a black overlay fading .25→.15 over 300ms. All use <code>(.4,0,.2,1)</code>.</p><p>A fixed clipping frame makes these tiny changes readable without shifting surrounding layout. Reference-title underlining is immediate. Motion within BLOC and APRIL is looping video content; their CSS hover scale is a separate effect.</p>'''+lab('media','Compare the zoom variants','The same artwork makes the changes in scale, starting crop, duration and overlay easy to compare.',hover,500,channel('scale','Image scale')+channel('overlay','Menu overlay'),'<div class="controls"><label>Recipe <select data-media-mode><option value="series">Series video · 1→1.03 / 500ms</option><option value="articles">Article · 1.02→1.04 / 300ms</option><option value="references">Reference · 1→1.02 / 300ms</option><option value="megaSeries">Mega thumbnail · 1→1.04 / 500ms</option></select></label></div>')+recipe('mediaHover',['b664f27371261aee.js','288d2982d73cba8c.js','a038fdf1b6baa266.js'])+'''<details><summary>Ambient video: what the DOM actually does</summary><p>The three native video elements are muted, looping, playsInline and preload=metadata. An effect calls play() unless reduced motion matches. Their autoplay attributes are false, but their runtime paused states are false because JavaScript starts playback. Vimeo progressive media supplies the files; these are not ambient player iframes.</p><p>At the footer, all three remained playing thousands of pixels above the viewport. AmbientVideo has no viewport pause observer and reads reduced motion at effect time without a preference-change subscription. Video edits, object movement and furniture morphs belong to the media itself. This offline study does not load those videos.</p></details>''')
gallery='<div class="gallery-window"><div class="gallery-track">'+''.join(f'<div class="gallery-tile tile-{i%4}"><span class="bench"></span><small>PUBLIC LIFE / {i%6+1:02}</small></div>' for i in range(18))+'</div></div>'
section('gallery','Continuous travel is a carousel engine.','''<p>The image strip uses Embla with loop, dragFree and start alignment, plus Auto Scroll with speed 0.5 and startDelay 0. This is 0.5px per 1000/60ms engine simulation step: 30px per second at steady state, with render interpolation. It is not a refresh-rate-dependent “0.5px every display frame” animation.</p><p>Pointer down pauses automatic travel. After release, the engine waits for the drag to settle and starts again. stopOnInteraction is false, stopOnMouseEnter defaults false, and stopOnFocusIn defaults true; focusout restarts it. Looping uses slide recycling rather than a two-copy CSS marquee. Desktop tiles follow their cropped natural aspect; narrow tiles use 3:4.</p><p>The homepage component has no reduced-motion guard or viewport-gated stop. A separate product-page gallery does have a reduced-motion check, but that does not establish the homepage’s behavior.</p>'''+lab('gallery','A steady 30px per second','This 12-second, finite study reproduces steady travel with substitute tiles. It does not reproduce Embla’s slide recycling, drag inertia or focus lifecycle.',gallery,12000)+recipe('gallery',['43f8342382a2e50d.js']))
overlap='<div class="overlap-board"><div class="overlap-card left"><div class="overlap-image tile-0"><span class="bench"></span></div><h4>Public belonging</h4></div><div class="overlap-card right"><div class="overlap-image tile-2"><span class="bench"></span></div><h4>Made in Scandinavia</h4></div></div>'
section('overlap','Scroll composes the two cards.','''<p>At ≥768px, both cards share a scroll target. Motion’s offsets are <code>["start 125vh", "end 35vh"]</code>: progress begins when the target’s top meets a line 1.25 viewport heights down, and ends when its bottom meets a line .35 viewport heights down. The values interpolate linearly and clamp at the ends.</p><p>The left/right horizontal transforms are +40%/−40% at progress 0 and both reach zero by .42. Vertically, +22px/−22px reach zero at .32, then separate to −40px/+36px at progress 1. The layout itself overlaps the columns and places the second card 192px lower. The percentages for x refer to each transformed element’s width.</p><p>Below 768px, each card has its own target and offsets <code>["start 105vh", "start 70vh"]</code>. The first enters from −14%, the second from +14%, both reaching zero. They therefore start at different page positions. useReducedMotion removes the transforms. There is no authored duration, spring or easing curve on this mapping.</p>'''+lab('overlap','Horizontal resolve + vertical separation','Scrub actual progress. Replay advances progress illustratively over 10 seconds; the source is driven by scroll position. Narrow mode uses a shared teaching slider for two independently triggered source targets.',overlap,10000,channel('x','Horizontal motion')+channel('y','Vertical motion'),'<div class="controls"><label>Source layout <select data-overlap-mode><option value="desktop">Desktop · shared target</option><option value="narrow">Narrow · independent targets</option></select></label></div>',kind='progress')+recipe('overlap',['43f8342382a2e50d.js']))
rowstage='<div class="native-row" tabindex="0" aria-label="Native scrolling cards">'+''.join(f'<article><div class="row-image tile-{i%4}"><span class="bench"></span></div><small>INSIGHT / {i+1:02}</small><h4>{title}</h4></article>' for i,title in enumerate(['Public life begins here','Places for everyone','Design with purpose','A lasting material','Made for the outdoors','Better together']))+'</div>'
rowlab='<div class="lab" id="lab-rows" data-lab="rows"><div class="lab-heading"><span class="eyebrow">Interactive study / rows</span><h3>Let the browser scroll and snap</h3><p>Native smooth scrolling and proximity snapping. The measured scroll position comes from this browser; no duration or easing is invented.</p></div><div class="stage rows-stage">'+rowstage+'</div><div class="controls"><button data-row-prev>← Previous</button><button data-row-next>Next →</button><button data-row-reset>Reset row</button></div><p class="readout" data-readout></p><p class="status" data-status aria-live="polite">Ready. Use buttons, trackpad, or the focusable row.</p></div>'
section('rows','Sometimes native scrolling is the animation.','''<p>Article and reference arrows add or subtract one container.clientWidth from scrollLeft. CSS supplies smooth scrolling, x-axis proximity snapping and snap-start children; the browser chooses the duration and interpolation, then clamps and snaps the destination. The live article row moved from 0 to 724px with a 725px maximum.</p><p>Scroll state updates in requestAnimationFrame and uses a 1px boundary tolerance. ResizeObserver recalculates overflow. Arrows disable at boundaries and disappear when no overflow exists. Article rows become a static vertical list of the first four below 768px; references continue horizontally. Coarse pointers hide the arrow controls. The demo stays horizontal to make the native mechanism inspectable.</p>'''+rowlab+recipe('rows',['43f8342382a2e50d.js']))
svg=(p/'footer.svg').read_text().replace('aria-hidden="false"','aria-hidden="true"')
footer='<div class="footer-window"><div class="footer-track">'+svg+svg+'</div></div>'
section('footer','Slow translation. Even slower rotation.','''<p>The footer track contains two identical SVG tiles and translates 0→−50% over 120 seconds, linearly and infinitely. At the seam, the second copy occupies the first copy’s position. A loop reset is invisible when the tiles and spacing match.</p><p>Inside each SVG, six groups rotate a complete turn in 450, 257, 600, 327, 225 and 400 seconds. Direction alternates normal/reverse. Each group uses transform-box:fill-box and a 50% 50% origin, so it turns around its own bounds. Strokes are 1.5px with non-scaling-stroke. Those different rates make the composition change slowly without a synchronized repeat every two minutes.</p><p>The live CSS inventory confirmed one track plus twelve rotating groups across the two SVG copies. Reduced-motion CSS disables both animations. No viewport stop is authored here. Bundled shape-morph keyframes were not attached to observed homepage elements; the footer is rotating paths, not morphing them.</p>'''+lab('footer','Original SVG, independent clocks','Original decorative SVG paths with source timing and origins. The 120-second slider covers one track lap, not a full repeat of every shape. Choose 10× playback to watch the slow systems.',footer,120000,channel('travel','Track translation')+channel('rotate','Six shape rotations'))+recipe('footer',['706408b83b455d97.css']))
mobile='<div class="device"><div class="device-base"><small>VESTRE / NARROW</small><h4>Furniture for public life.</h4></div><div class="mobile-backdrop"></div><div class="mobile-sheet"><div class="mobile-bar"><b>vestre</b><span class="mobile-state-label">MENU / CLOSE ×</span></div><div class="mobile-panels"><div class="mobile-root"><p>PRODUCTS →</p><p>PROJECTS →</p><p>ABOUT →</p></div><div class="mobile-sub"><small>← BACK / PRODUCTS</small><p>Seating</p><p>Tables</p><p>Planters</p></div></div></div></div>'
section('mobile','Two axes, two states.','''<p>The narrow menu is a Base UI Dialog with keepMounted portal content. The full-screen sheet slides down from −100% y to zero over 300ms with <code>(0,0,.2,1)</code>. Its backdrop fades over 150ms using <code>(.4,0,.2,1)</code>. Links arrive together.</p><p>Products drill-down sends the root panel from x=0 to −100% and the submenu from +100% to zero over 300ms with the same slide curve. Back reverses them. Icons and retained submenu content swap discretely. This was inspected at 390px with a fine pointer; physical touch gestures were not tested.</p>'''+lab('mobile','Open, close, drill and return','The source’s viewport-sized menu is shown in a contained device. Choose a state transition and replay it.',mobile,300,channel('sheet','Sheet / panel transforms')+channel('backdrop','Backdrop fade'),'<div class="controls"><label>Transition <select data-mobile-mode><option value="open">Open menu</option><option value="close">Close menu</option><option value="drill">Products drill-down</option><option value="back">Back to root</option></select></label></div>')+recipe('mobile',['b664f27371261aee.js']))
drawer='<div class="drawer-canvas"><div class="drawer-under"><small>VESTRE / SEARCH</small><h4>Explore public life.</h4></div><div class="drawer-backdrop"></div><div class="drawer-sheet"><small>SEARCH / CLOSE ×</small><h4>What are you looking for?</h4><div class="fake-input">Search products, stories and places</div><p>Try GLADE, VROOM or Sustainability</p></div></div>'
section('drawer','A fast start and a soft landing.','''<p>Search and floating help/project controls use a side drawer. Its closed transform is 100% of its width plus viewport padding: 8px normally, 10px in the WebKit rule. Opening takes 450ms with <code>(.32,.72,0,1)</code>. The backdrop follows the same transition; its destination alpha is .2 in light mode or .7 in the OS dark rule.</p><p>Closing uses 400ms multiplied by --drawer-swipe-strength. The inspected ordinary close uses strength=1, so 400ms. While swiping, translation follows displacement directly with transitions disabled; release timing depends on swipe strength. The study covers ordinary open/close, not the untested physical swipe.</p>'''+lab('drawer','Drawer + scrim','Compare the 450ms opening and 400ms ordinary closing. Select a backdrop theme; the popup artwork remains a teaching substitute.',drawer,450,channel('slide','Popup slide')+channel('scrim','Backdrop'),'<div class="controls"><label>Direction <select data-drawer-mode><option value="open">Open · 450ms</option><option value="close">Close · 400ms</option></select></label><label>Backdrop <select data-drawer-theme><option value="light">Light · .2</option><option value="dark">Dark · .7</option></select></label></div>')+recipe('drawer',['706408b83b455d97.css','2ed703a53512c6bb.js']))
placeholder='<div class="placeholder-canvas"><small>SEARCH PLACEHOLDER</small><div class="placeholder-window" aria-hidden="true"><div class="placeholder-old"></div><div class="placeholder-new"></div></div><label class="query-label">Try typing locally <input data-local-query type="text" autocomplete="off" placeholder="A query hides the animated hint"></label></div>'
section('placeholder','The input becomes a tiny type stage.','''<p>With multiple hints, an empty query and reduced motion off, AnimatedPlaceholders advances every 3000ms. AnimatePresence overlaps outgoing and incoming strings. Characters enter from +150% y and leave toward −150%, taking 500ms with <code>(.33,1,.68,1)</code>. Delay is 20ms × non-space character index + 10ms × word index.</p><p>The clipped input window hides both outside positions. Typing a query hides the animated hint immediately. Reduced motion selects a static first hint and skips the interval. The live search cycled between Sustainability and VROOM. This study isolates one replacement; it does not run an automatic timer or send your query anywhere.</p>'''+lab('placeholder','Outgoing and incoming letters','Watch both strings in the same clipped window, or hide the mask. The stage uses the observed strings with substituted type.',placeholder,800,channel('exit','Outgoing characters')+channel('enter','Incoming characters')+channel('stagger','Character delays')+channel('mask','Input mask'))+recipe('placeholder',['b664f27371261aee.js']))
help='<div class="help-canvas"><small>ONE-TIME GREETING / LAUNCHER</small><div class="help-launcher"><span class="help-greeting"><span>Request a quote or talk to us</span></span><b>↗</b></div><div class="help-time-note">3s wait → 6s visible → close</div></div>'
section('help','A delayed invitation, then quiet.','''<p>The floating launcher waits 3000ms before showing its greeting, keeps it open for 6000ms, then closes around 9000ms. A grid track transitions from 0fr to 1fr over 300ms with <code>(0,0,.2,1)</code>; its child clips overflow. This sizes the text without guessing a fixed text width. The 44px icon stays available.</p><p>An IntersectionObserver watches the site footer. When it intersects and the drawer is closed, the launcher fades out over 200ms with Tailwind’s default curve and stops receiving pointer input. It does not hide an already open drawer. The live footer inspection confirmed launcher opacity zero.</p>'''+lab('help','Wait, expand, hold, collapse','Scrub directly to 3000ms or use Inspect greeting to skip the initial wait. Footer visibility is a separate state transition.',help,9300,'','<div class="controls"><button data-help-inspect>Inspect greeting</button><label><input type="checkbox" data-help-footer>Footer intersects / drawer closed</label></div>')+recipe('help',['b664f27371261aee.js']))
section('building','How to build with this vocabulary.','''<p><strong>Choose the mechanism to fit the clock.</strong> CSS transitions handle reversible hover and menu states. Motion is useful for mount choreography, character staggering, AnimatePresence and scroll transforms. Embla handles drag, inertia and loop recycling. Native scrolling provides the row’s behavior without a custom tween. None requires Framer Motion to animate a simple opacity, but coordination and lifecycle are where a motion library earns its place.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Clock</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Useful implementation lesson</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mount</td><td>Hero media + letters + support</td><td>Calculate delays from the last character’s start. Overlap the sequences.</td></tr><tr><td>Interaction state</td><td>CSS transitions / Base UI</td><td>Animate fixed containers. Preserve dismissal, focus and content lifecycle.</td></tr><tr><td>Scroll position</td><td>Motion useScroll / useTransform</td><td>Map clamped progress; do not replace with a one-shot entrance.</td></tr><tr><td>Continuous time</td><td>Embla + CSS footer loops</td><td>Use an engine for drag/recycling; a two-copy track for the decorative repeat.</td></tr><tr><td>Browser physics</td><td>Native smooth scroll + snap</td><td>Do not pretend its duration is an authored constant.</td></tr><tr><td>Media time</td><td>Native video</td><td>Keep footage movement separate from the container’s DOM effects.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><strong>Keep transforms and opacity small and purposeful.</strong> Hover zooms are only 2–4%; character movement is clipped; cards converge before their vertical separation becomes noticeable. The hero’s 2px blur and header’s backdrop blur still deserve measurement, and height/grid transitions can require layout. Source inspection alone cannot certify a compositor path or smooth frame rate.</p><p><strong>Improve the lifecycle when rebuilding.</strong> Pause videos and continuous galleries when outside the viewport or document is hidden. Subscribe to preference changes. Make hover information equally available to keyboard/touch users. Give reduced motion a stable destination. This showcase applies those teaching safeguards globally; the original has selective guards, not one blanket policy.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Original component</th><th>Reduced-motion evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Overlapping cards</td><td>useReducedMotion removes transforms.</td></tr><tr><td>Footer SVG</td><td>CSS disables track and shape animation.</td></tr><tr><td>Animated placeholder</td><td>Static first hint; no interval.</td></tr><tr><td>Ambient video</td><td>Preference checked at effect start; no change subscription.</td></tr><tr><td>Hero, header, navigation, product hovers, homepage gallery, greeting</td><td>No component-local reduced-motion guard found for the documented behavior. OS preference changes were not exercised on the live site.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>''')
images=[('live-hero.png','Live homepage: final hero state. Initial letter/media values were separately read during reload.'),('live-mega-menu.png','Live desktop Products menu opened with keyboard focus.'),('live-product-hover.png','Live Fari pointer hover: lifestyle layer shown, caption hidden.'),('live-story-cards.png','Live overlapping story cards after scroll.'),('live-footer.png','Live footer: the translating SVG and independent rotating groups.'),('live-search.png','Live search drawer and its animated hint.'),('live-mobile-menu.png','390px fine-pointer viewport: root navigation.'),('live-mobile-submenu.png','390px viewport: Products drill-down destination.')]
figures=''.join(f'<figure><a href="{name}"><img src="{name}" alt="{html.escape(caption)}" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>{caption}</figcaption></figure>' for name,caption in images)
section('evidence','What was verified, and what is a reconstruction.','''<p>The investigation combined live scrolling, pointer hover, keyboard focus, search/menu dismissal, computed styles, DOM geometry and the delivered first-party JavaScript/CSS. Fifty-three public asset files are archived with URLs, byte counts and SHA-256 hashes. Prefetched product modules are present in that archive; presence in a bundle does not prove an effect runs on the homepage.</p><p>Live checks confirmed the header endpoints, initial hero offsets, image-layer opacities, series scale, card transforms, native row travel, narrow menu panels, drawer easing, placeholder changes, footer animation rates, footer-triggered help hiding and offscreen video playback. Screenshots document visible states; they are not timing traces. Exact durations and stagger formulas come from the source and matching computed styles.</p><p>The offline regions reconstruct the animation rules with substitute artwork, fonts and dimensions, except for the original decorative footer paths. They are teaching models, not pixel-identical page copies. The gallery study does not implement Embla inertia; narrow card progress is jointly scrubbed for explanation; row easing remains browser-native. Physical touch swipes, account/forms, product configurators, full navigation transitions and frame-time/FPS traces are outside this homepage audit.</p><p>No general scroll-triggered fade/rise was observed on the static intro or article/reference wrappers. No active homepage path morph was found. The homepage is relatively restrained: precision comes from matching each mechanism to its role, not from adding a reveal to every block.</p><div class="source-links"><a href="homepage-motion-data.json">Structured motion values</a><a href="evidence/motion-source-index.txt">Source symbol index</a><a href="evidence/asset-manifest.json">Asset manifest + hashes</a><a href="evidence/live-runtime.json">Live runtime values</a><a href="verification.json">Showcase verification</a><a href="README.md">Files and rebuild instructions</a></div>'''+figures)
